WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.966
A Survey of Instructional Design Models.

2
00:00:03.966 --> 00:00:10.213
ID 553 - Module 1: Introduction to Instructional Design
Models.

3
00:00:10.213 --> 00:00:16.162
Presented by Idowu David Awoyemi, The University of Alabama.

4
00:00:16.762 --> 00:00:25.737
An instructional design model is a representation of the
design process: what to do, in what order, and what each
step produces.

5
00:00:25.737 --> 00:00:27.350
Models do three things.

6
00:00:27.350 --> 00:00:31.137
They communicate the process to clients and teammates.

7
00:00:31.137 --> 00:00:35.204
They structure the work so nothing essential gets skipped.

8
00:00:35.204 --> 00:00:45.512
And they embed assumptions about learning and about how
design should proceed, which is why choosing a model is a
design decision in its own right.

9
00:00:45.512 --> 00:00:55.960
To keep this concrete, I'll run one example through every
model: retraining four hundred nurses on a new records
system with eleven weeks to go-live.

10
00:00:56.560 --> 00:01:07.089
ADDIE is less a specific model than a family name for the
generic systems approach: analysis, design, development,
implementation, evaluation.

11
00:01:07.089 --> 00:01:29.334
For the nurses, analysis means finding out what they know,
when they can train, and which workflows fail; design means
objectives and assessments; development means building
modules and job aids; implementation means delivering them
across three shifts; evaluation means help desk data after
go-live.

12
00:01:29.334 --> 00:01:32.968
ADDIE's strength is that everyone understands it.

13
00:01:32.968 --> 00:01:40.086
Its weakness is that it's often practiced as a rigid
waterfall with evaluation stuck at the end.

14
00:01:40.086 --> 00:01:43.052
Most later models are responses to that.

15
00:01:43.652 --> 00:01:55.842
Dick and Carey's Systems Approach Model is the most detailed
articulation of the systems view: ten components from
identifying instructional goals through summative
evaluation.

16
00:01:55.842 --> 00:01:57.573
Two features matter most.

17
00:01:57.573 --> 00:02:04.846
Objectives are written before assessments, and assessments
before strategies, which guarantees alignment.

18
00:02:04.846 --> 00:02:10.941
And revision is a loop fed by formative evaluation that
touches every earlier component.

19
00:02:10.941 --> 00:02:22.438
For the nurses, Dick and Carey would insist on a task
analysis of medication administration and a performance test
before anyone chooses between classroom and online.

20
00:02:22.438 --> 00:02:25.554
This is the model behind most of this course.

21
00:02:26.154 --> 00:02:42.237
The Kemp model presents nine elements in a circle rather
than a line: instructional problems, learner
characteristics, task analysis, objectives, sequencing,
strategies, message design, delivery, and evaluation
instruments.

22
00:02:42.237 --> 00:02:49.017
The circle signals that you can start anywhere and that
everything depends on everything else.

23
00:02:49.017 --> 00:02:56.518
Around the circle sit ongoing processes: planning, project
management, support services, and evaluation.

24
00:02:56.518 --> 00:03:06.182
Kemp fits classroom teachers well, and it fits projects like
the nurses' training where constraints, not objectives,
drive the design.

25
00:03:06.782 --> 00:03:11.647
Michael Allen's Successive Approximation Model is the agile
alternative.

26
00:03:11.647 --> 00:03:22.932
After a short preparation phase, the project cycles through
iterative design and iterative development, with
stakeholders reviewing working prototypes early and often.

27
00:03:22.932 --> 00:03:31.649
The core belief is that a rough prototype reviewed in week
two teaches the team more than a design document approved in
week six.

28
00:03:31.649 --> 00:03:38.541
For the nurses, SAM would have a playable practice module in
front of a few nurses within a fortnight.

29
00:03:38.541 --> 00:03:42.325
It fits poorly where sign-off must precede any building.

30
00:03:42.925 --> 00:03:55.353
Understanding by Design, from Wiggins and McTighe, is a
curriculum framework rather than a full process model, but
its three stages reorganize how designers think.

31
00:03:55.353 --> 00:03:57.259
Identify desired results.

32
00:03:57.259 --> 00:03:59.546
Determine acceptable evidence.

33
00:03:59.546 --> 00:04:03.129
Then, and only then, plan learning experiences.

34
00:04:03.129 --> 00:04:05.035
The insight is the order.

35
00:04:05.035 --> 00:04:15.023
Teachers frequently plan activities first and assessments
last, which produces engaging lessons that don't teach what
was intended.

36
00:04:15.023 --> 00:04:22.113
Backward Design shares this commitment with Dick and Carey
but says it in classroom language.

37
00:04:22.713 --> 00:04:29.439
David Merrill reviewed the major instructional theories and
extracted five principles they share.

38
00:04:29.439 --> 00:04:46.636
Learning is promoted when learners are engaged in solving
real-world problems; when existing knowledge is activated as
a foundation; when new knowledge is demonstrated; when the
learner applies it; and when it's integrated into the
learner's world.

39
00:04:46.636 --> 00:04:49.340
First Principles isn't a process model.

40
00:04:49.340 --> 00:04:52.392
It's a checklist to hold any design against.

41
00:04:52.392 --> 00:04:55.304
A lecture that only demonstrates fails it.

42
00:04:55.304 --> 00:04:58.840
An unguided project that only applies fails it too.

43
00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:11.599
For the nurses: does the training start with a real patient
scenario, activate what they already do, demonstrate the new
workflow, have them practice it, and connect it to their
shift?

44
00:05:12.199 --> 00:05:13.586
How do you choose?

45
00:05:13.586 --> 00:05:15.050
Ask four questions.

46
00:05:15.050 --> 00:05:17.516
How well defined is the problem?

47
00:05:17.516 --> 00:05:24.373
Well defined favors Dick and Carey or ADDIE; ill defined
favors SAM or rapid prototyping.

48
00:05:24.373 --> 00:05:27.763
How available are stakeholders and learners?

49
00:05:27.763 --> 00:05:30.305
Iterative models need them often.

50
00:05:30.305 --> 00:05:35.544
What does the client expect, a design document or something
working?

51
00:05:35.544 --> 00:05:38.626
And what's the risk of getting it wrong?

52
00:05:38.626 --> 00:05:41.477
High-stakes performance favors rigor.

53
00:05:41.477 --> 00:05:51.570
In practice, most designers use an ADDIE skeleton with
iterative development inside it and Merrill's principles as
a quality check.

54
00:05:51.570 --> 00:05:57.811
That hybrid is a legitimate answer in this course, as long
as you can justify it.

55
00:05:57.811 --> 00:06:02.511
Bring your project problem and your choice to the
discussion.
